solvable, imported EVPN routes cannot forward.</p><h4>Troubleshooting</h4><p>Check VSX peer/ISL state, both BGP EVPN sessions, the route to the logical VTEP, VXLAN tunnel state, endpoint MAC/ARP/ND, and actual packet forwarding. Compare both chassis rather than treating the pair as a single debugging object.</p><h4>Interoperability</h4><p>Cisco, Arista, Juniper, or FRR remote VTEPs do not need to understand VSX internals. Their interoperability contract is standards-based EVPN information plus IP reachability to the advertised VTEP next hop.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show bgp l2vpn evpn
show evpn
show vsx status
show ip route &lt;logical-vtep&gt;</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 13, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Aruba/HPE</span><h3>14. OSPF redistribution and active-routes-only</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.15/PDF/ip_route_6300-6400-8100-83xx-9300-10000.pdf">https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.15/PDF/ip_route_6300-6400-8100-83xx-9300-10000.pdf</a></p><h4>Redistribution source set</h4><p>AOS-CX OSPF can redistribute routes from sources such as BGP, connected, static, RIP, and other routing processes, optionally controlled by route maps. The operator must distinguish a route that exists in a protocol database from one that is eligible to be redistributed.</p><h4>Active-routes-only behavior</h4><p>AOS-CX documents `route-redistribute active-routes-only`, which restricts candidates to routes selected as active for forwarding. If the same prefix is learned from BGP and static, only the winning route may become a redistribution candidate depending on the feature state.</p><h4>Failure diagnosis</h4><p>A route can be visible under BGP yet absent from OSPF externals because it lost RIB selection—not because the route map denied it. Check active RIB state before debugging the redistribution policy.</p><h4>OSPF output</h4><p>After candidate selection and route-map evaluation, verify the resulting external LSA and how it propagates through area types. Confirm metric and metric type as well as prefix presence.</p><h4>Cross-vendor lesson</h4><p>Some platforms redistribute from a protocol’s known routes while others effectively operate from active RIB candidates by default or by option. Record that behavior explicitly when migrating designs.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show ip route
show route-map
show ip ospf database external</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 14, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Aruba/HPE</span><h3>15. Layered VXLAN troubleshooting with VSX</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.15/PDF/vsx.pdf">https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.15/PDF/vsx.pdf</a></p><h4>Why layered troubleshooting fits VSX EVPN</h4><p>A VSX EVPN fabric naturally contains several independent layers: host-facing multichassis attachment, underlay IP reachability, two BGP EVPN speakers, a shared VTEP identity, tenant VNI/VRF state, and endpoint learning.</p><h4>Recommended order</h4><p>Start with physical/VSX/LACP state, then prove underlay route to the logical VTEP, then BGP EVPN adjacency and route import, then VNI/VRF mapping, then endpoint MAC/ARP/ND, then actual packet forwarding. This order follows dependencies rather than vendor menus.</p><h4>Common misdiagnosis</h4><p>If the logical VTEP route is missing, spending time on EVPN RT policy is premature. If the underlay and EVPN routes are correct but one dual-homed server fails, move down to VSX/LACP/VLAN state.</p><h4>Multivendor use</h4><p>The same sequence works when the remote fabric is Cisco or Arista because the dependency graph is standards-based: IP reachability first, EVPN control plane next, VXLAN data plane after that.</p><h4>Lab method</h4><p>Introduce one failure at each layer and record which show command changes first. Over time this builds a symptom-to-boundary map that is more valuable than memorizing isolated commands.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show vsx status
show ip route &lt;logical-vtep&gt;
show bgp l2vpn evpn
show evpn</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 15, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Juniper</span><h3>16. EVPN-VXLAN with IPv6 underlay</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/evpn/topics/topic-map/vxlan-ipv6-underlay-overview.html">https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/evpn/topics/topic-map/vxlan-ipv6-underlay-overview.html</a></p><h4>IPv6 underlay model</h4><p>Juniper documents EVPN-VXLAN fabrics where VTEP reachability uses IPv6 rather than IPv4. The overlay still uses EVPN route types and VXLAN; the outer tunnel header and underlay routing state change address family.</p><h4>Supported underlay protocols and route types</h4><p>Juniper qualifies iBGP/eBGP and OSPFv3 for the IPv6 underlay on supported platforms. Type 1/2/3/4 routes are broadly supported, with Type-5 support on specified platform families. Platform and release matrices matter because optional feature combinations are not universal.</p><h4>Important limitations</h4><p>The documentation warns that IPv4 and IPv6 underlays cannot simply be mixed across EVPN instances in one fabric, and IPv6-underlay support is tied to MAC-VRF/VXLAN designs on supported systems. Some platforms require system-profile or tunnel-termination specifics.</p><h4>Control/data-plane consequence</h4><p>A remote EVPN route can be correct while the IPv6 route to the remote VTEP is missing. Troubleshooting therefore shifts recursive lookups and neighbor/forwarding evidence into the IPv6 underlay without changing the basic overlay mental model.</p><h4>MTU and migration</h4><p>The outer IPv6 header changes encapsulation overhead compared with IPv4. Validate MTU end-to-end. When migrating an existing IPv4 underlay, remove incompatible underlay-specific configuration and follow platform reboot/profile requirements rather than treating the change as an address-only edit.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show route &lt;remote-vtep-ipv6&gt;
show bgp summary
show evpn database</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 16, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Juniper</span><h3>17. Routing protocols on EVPN IRB</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/evpn/topics/concept/protocols-evpn-vxlan.html">https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/evpn/topics/concept/protocols-evpn-vxlan.html</a></p><h4>IRB as a routing-protocol boundary</h4><p>Junos supports OSPF, IS-IS, and BGP adjacencies on IRB interfaces in EVPN-VXLAN designs. That means an IRB can serve both as a first-hop gateway for endpoints and as a routed handoff toward a firewall, load balancer, CE, or other service device.</p><h4>Two independent control planes</h4><p>The local OSPF/BGP/IS-IS adjacency on the IRB is separate from the EVPN overlay. An IRB neighbor can be healthy while remote VNI import is broken, or EVPN can be healthy while the attached routing neighbor is down because of BFD, timers, authentication, or interface policy.</p><h4>Troubleshooting</h4><p>Prove local IRB interface state and routing adjacency, local RIB installation, redistribution/advertisement into EVPN if applicable, remote route import, and VTEP recursion. Avoid assuming an adjacency problem merely because the end-to-end tenant path fails.</p><h4>Integration use case</h4><p>This is a common place to connect Palo Alto or FortiGate appliances to a fabric. The firewall may run eBGP/OSPF to the IRB while the switching fabric uses EVPN internally.</p><h4>Design caution</h4><p>Be explicit about where route ownership changes. If the IRB learns external routes and also participates in overlay routing, apply filtering and provenance controls to prevent accidental feedback between the attached protocol and EVPN.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show ospf neighbor
show isis adjacency
show bgp summary
show bfd session</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 17, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Juniper</span><h3>18. Type-5 subnet routes for scale</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/release-notes/25.4/junos-release-notes-25.4r1/topics/new-features/feature-descriptions/evpn-9.html">https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/release-notes/25.4/junos-release-notes-25.4r1/topics/new-features/feature-descriptions/evpn-9.html</a></p><h4>Scale problem</h4><p>Large stretched VXLAN fabrics can create excessive host-specific routing state if every endpoint prefix is propagated to every tier. Juniper’s newer Type-5 capabilities and policy options let designers keep host detail where it is operationally useful while advertising subnet-level reachability to other layers.</p><h4>Abstraction boundary</h4><p>A Type-5 subnet route tells a receiving device how to reach a prefix without requiring one route per host. This reduces BGP state, RIB/FIB entries, update churn, and convergence work on tiers that do not need endpoint granularity.</p><h4>Troubleshooting tradeoff</h4><p>With summarization or subnet-only advertisement, an individual host outage does not necessarily correspond to a missing route at every layer. The edge may retain the subnet route while the endpoint-specific state is missing closer to the access layer.</p><h4>Design method</h4><p>Document which tier owns host specificity, which tier advertises subnet